{
  "dom_id": "step-q114069",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/938babc27340209a1a5477fb3184abbf155f409c520c76995c4e234f99691347.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=-f(\\frac{x}{3})?$",
  "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=-f(\\frac{x}{3}),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=3$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{x}{3}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/f0b289d42620a275eeb90d4bdbe265ca92ea4264de70ecc11e5f661a84149628.png)\n\n- Then, reflect the result across the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-f(\\frac{x}{3}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/597ba19a0c993ea597ecd091df02d97e974364125c79780df04aef365442abb6.png)",
